#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Package versions\">\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The code on this page was developed using the following requirements.\n"
"We recommend using these versions or newer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"The Executor primitive is part of the [directed execution model](/guides/directed-execution-model), which provides more flexibility when customizing an error mitigation workflow.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The inputs and outputs of the Executor primitive are very different from "
"those of the [Sampler](/guides/sampler-input-output) and "
"[Estimator](/guides/estimator-input-output) primitives. For example, instead"
" of taking a list of PUBs as the input, Executor takes a `QuantumProgram`, "
"which contains a list of `QuantumProgramItem` objects. These container "
"classes give you more flexibility than a PUB, which is a simple tuple data "
"structure.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Executor's output is a `QuantumProgramResult`, which is an iterable and "
"contains one element for each input `QuantumProgramItem`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"programs\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Inputs: Quantum programs {#inputs-quantum-programs}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As stated previously, the input to an Executor primitive is a  [`QuantumProgram`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-ibm-runtime/quantum-program-quantum-program), which is an iterable of\n"
"[`QuantumProgramItem`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-ibm-runtime/quantum-program-quantum-program-item) objects.  These objects can be of two types:\n"
"- `CircuitItem`, which typically stores a circuit and its parameter values (if any).\n"
"- `SamplexItem`, which typically stores the following:\n"
"    - A template circuit\n"
"    - A samplex object, which is used to generate randomized sets of parameters at runtime (for example to perform twirling or inject noise)\n"
"    - Arguments for the samplex, which might include parameter values for the original circuit\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Each of these items represents a different task for Executor to perform.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Before you begin {#before-you-begin}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Some of the code examples on this page use `samplex`, which is part of the "
"Samplomatic package.  Therefore, before running those code block, you must "
"install Samplomatic, as shown in the following code block.  For more "
"information, see the [Samplomatic "
"documentation](https://qiskit.github.io/samplomatic).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Example: Create a `QuantumProgram` with two different tasks {#example-"
"create-a-quantumprogram-with-two-different-tasks}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"First initialize your quantum program, then append program items to it by "
"using either `append_circuit_item` or `append_samplex_item` (if a samplex is"
" present), as shown in the following examples.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following cell initializes a `QuantumProgram` and specifies that it "
"should run 1024 shots for every configuration of each item in the program.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"note\">\n"
"Unlike with Sampler, a `QuantumProgram` takes only a single shot value. If you want a different shot value, you need a separate `QuantumProgram`, which would be a separate job.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Append a `CircuitItem` {#append-a-circuititem}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Next, append the target circuit, which was transpiled according to the "
"backend's instruction set architecture (ISA), to the `QuantumProgram`. Since"
" this circuit has two parameters, we must also provide the parameter values "
"(10 sets in this example). Running this `CircuitItem` is the first task that"
" the program will perform.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Append a `SamplexItem` {#append-a-samplexitem}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Circuit items are executed without any sort of randomization. On the "
"contrary, samplex items let you specify how to randomize their content. The "
"next cell uses the `generate_boxing_pass_manager()` function to group the "
"circuit's gates and measurements into boxes and add a twirling annotation to"
" each box. It then generates a template circuit and a samplex pair using the"
" `build()` function.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Running this `SamplexItem` is the second task that the program will "
"perform.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"See the Samplomatic [API](https://github.com/Qiskit/samplomatic/) "
"documentation for full details about `samplex` and its arguments. See the "
"Samplomatic [Transpiler "
"guide](https://qiskit.github.io/samplomatic/guides/transpiler.html) for "
"information about using the  `generate_boxing_pass_manager()` function.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Outputs {#outputs}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Executor's output is a "
"[`QuantumProgramResult`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-ibm-"
"runtime/results-quantum-program-result), which is an iterable. It contains "
"one entry per input `QuantumProgramItem` in the same order as the input "
"items. Each of these output items is a dictionary where the keys are strings"
" that correspond the classical registers' names in the input circuits (among"
" others), so you no longer need to memorize these names like you did with "
"Sampler output. The dictionary values are of type `np.ndarray`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The result for the previous example contains these items:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`CircuitItem` result {#circuititem-result}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The first item contains the results of running the first task (a "
"`CircuitItem`) in the program. It contains a single key, `meas`, which is "
"the name of the classical register in the input circuit. The value of this "
"key maps to an `np.ndarray` of shape `(parameter sets, shots, register "
"bits)`, which is (10, 1024, 3) for the above example.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following code illustrates how to access this information:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The right-most axis of `result_0` runs over classical bits in little-endian "
"order. Thus, index 0 holds the outcome of classical bit 0, the least-"
"significant bit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`SamplexItem` result {#samplexitem-result}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The second item contains the results of running the second task (a "
"`SamplexItem`) in the program. This item contains multiple keys. The `meas` "
"key, which is the name of the input circuit's classical register, maps to "
"that register's array of results. This array has the shape `(randomizations,"
" parameter sets, shots, classical bits)`, or (28, 10, 1024, 3) in this "
"example.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Additionally, the output contains a `measurement_flips.meas` key, which is "
"the bit-flip corrections to undo the measurement twirling for the `meas` "
"register.  This output shape will be (28, 10, 1, 3) for our example because "
"only one shot is required to perform the bit-flip.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Note that the right-most axis of `result_1` runs over classical bits in "
"little-endian order.\n"
msgstr ""

