msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "View execution spans {#view-execution-spans}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A single `Executor.run()` call submits a single job. On the server side, "
"however, the service does not necessarily run every configuration of the "
"quantum program at the same time. Instead, it groups the program items' "
"bound circuits into _chunks_, and runs one chunk at a time on the QPU. You "
"don't need to split your program or submit additional jobs; the chunking "
"happens automatically within the one job. The result's "
"[`metadata`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-ibm-runtime/results-"
"metadata) attribute contains timing information about these chunks in "
"`result.metadata.chunk_timing`. These timestamps are the actual start and "
"stop times of each chunk on the QPU. You can use this information to see "
"when particular data was collected on the QPU, to understand how Executor "
"distributed the work, or to diagnose performance issues.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`chunk_timing` is a list that contains one "
"[`ChunkSpan`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-ibm-runtime/results-"
"chunk-span) per executed chunk. Each `ChunkSpan` has the following "
"attributes:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`start` and `stop`: The UTC timestamps when the chunk started and stopped "
"executing on the QPU.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`parts`: A list of [`ChunkPart`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"ibm-runtime/results-chunk-part) objects that describe which program items "
"contributed to the chunk. Each `ChunkPart` contains the following:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '    - '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`idx_item`: The index of the program item, in the order that the items were "
"appended to the `QuantumProgram`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '    - '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`size`: The number of configurations (elements of the item's shape) from "
"that item that were executed in this chunk. For example, the `SamplexItem` "
"in the previous example has shape `(28, 10)`, so it has 280 configurations "
"in total.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following code prints the duration of each chunk and the contents of "
"each chunk for the job submitted previously:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The `QuantumProgramResult.timing` property wraps the same list in a "
"[`ChunkTiming`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-ibm-runtime/results-"
"chunk-timing) object, which also reports global information about the "
"collection of chunks:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Visualize execution spans {#visualize-execution-spans}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Use the [`draw_chunk_timings`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-ibm-"
"runtime/visualization-draw-chunk-timings) function to visualize the chunk "
"timings on an interactive bar plot. The x-axis is time, and each chunk is "
"drawn as a horizontal segment that spans its start and stop times. The "
"y-axis shows the cumulative number of configurations completed (or the "
"percentage of the total workload, if you set `normalize_y=True`). Hover over"
" a segment to see the timestamps, duration, and parts of that chunk.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The function returns a `plotly` figure, which you can display directly, save"
" to a file, or both. It accepts either the `chunk_timing` list or a "
"`ChunkTiming` object. You can pass several of them, for example from "
"different jobs, to draw them on the same plot. Set `common_start=True` to "
"align them at a common start time for comparison.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"note\" title=\"Plotly required\">\n"
"`draw_chunk_timings` requires `plotly`. You can install it by running `pip install plotly`.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"note\" title=\"Gaps between chunks\">\n"
"The visualization might show gaps between the chunk execution. All jobs submitted to a real QPU go through some classical preprocessing, such as translation into pulses that can be sent to the hardware. Because this preprocessing can be done in parallel, the service dequeues and starts multiple jobs simultaneously. The QPU, however, can only process one unit of work at a time. Therefore, if a job generates multiple chunks, its chunks might be interleaved with those of other users' jobs, and each chunk might have to wait for its turn on the QPU. This is why gaps can appear between chunks.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

